%I A398259 #33 Jul 31 2026 14:05:57
%S A398259 0,0,1,0,2,0,2,2,1,6,0,5,0,2,6,5,4,0,5,3,0,3,2,9,0,4,9,3,6,14,11,8,0,
%T A398259 8,2,12,8,3,10,30,2,6,13,17,7,0,13,21,10,40,24,9,25,8,17,1,47,26,4,33,
%U A398259 18,9,10,7,19,2,25,3,30,1,14,52,8,19,11,9,14,58,31,20
%N A398259 a(1)=0; for n>=2, let s = digit sum of a(n-1); if s=a(m) for some m < n-1, let m be the greatest such index and set a(n)=n-1-m; otherwise set a(n) = 0.
%C A398259 Van Eck-like sequence using the sum of the decimal digits of the previous term as lookup key. It agrees with Van Eck's sequence A181391 for the first 30 terms a(1)..a(30). The sequences first differ at a(31): A181391 has 0, this sequence has 11, because digsum(a(30)) = digsum(14) = 5, and 5 last appeared at index 19, giving distance 30-19=11.
%C A398259 Among the first 10^6 terms there are 26 zeros, see A398307. No further zero was found through n=10^6. It is conjectured that these are all the zeros.
%C A398259 Empirically, a(n)/n appears to approach 1. In particular, a(10^4)=9365, a(10^5)=99353 and a(10^6)=999847. Conjecture: lim_{n->oo} a(n)/n=1.
%H A398259 Bence Bernáth, <a href="/A398259/b398259.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%e A398259 a(30)=14, digsum of 14 is 5, the most recent occurrence of 5 was at a(19), so a(31)=30-19=11.
%o A398259 (Python)
%o A398259 def A398259_list(n):
%o A398259     if n <= 0:
%o A398259         return []
%o A398259     seq = [0]
%o A398259     term_last = {}
%o A398259     for ii in range(n - 1):
%o A398259         v = seq[ii]
%o A398259         s = sum(int(ch) for ch in str(v))
%o A398259         new = (ii - term_last[s]) if s in term_last else 0
%o A398259         term_last[v] = ii
%o A398259         seq.append(new)
%o A398259     return seq
%Y A398259 Cf. A007953, A181391, A398260, A398307.
%K A398259 nonn,base,easy,new
%O A398259 1,5
%A A398259 _Bence Bernáth_, Jul 24 2026
